From: fpstudio
Subject: [PATCH] goodix55x4: let a preview session still confirm a touch

The private live preview wrote each frame and then scheduled its own re-read,
returning TRUE so the finger-detect handler stopped there. Nothing downstream
ran: not the poll-again path, and not the branch that reports a confirmed touch
and advances libfprint to capture.

That was invisible while preview was only used to collect research captures,
because a capture session never needs a touch to be confirmed - it reads frames
and picks the good ones itself. It matters for enrolment. With the preview path
active the window shows the finger and builds its coverage map exactly as
before, while libfprint advances no enrol stage at all, so the session looks
like it is working and produces no enrolment. Enrolling and mapping could not
be the same act.

The frame is now recorded and handed back: the existing logic below decides
whether to poll again or to confirm the touch. Two consequences, both
deliberate:

  - Preview frames arrive at the finger-detect poll interval rather than the
    1ms loop, so the window sees roughly 10 frames a second where it saw 15.
  - The ten-second empty-sensor failure is not counted while a preview is on
    screen. During a verify an empty sensor means the prompt went unanswered;
    during a preview the person is looking at the window and deciding where to
    press next, and the session already has its own deadline.

--- a/libfprint/drivers/goodixtls/goodix55x4.c
+++ b/libfprint/drivers/goodixtls/goodix55x4.c
@@ -924,10 +924,6 @@
   fpi_ssm_jump_to_state(ssm, SCAN_STAGE_SWITCH_TO_FDT_MODE);
 }
 
-static void preview_next_frame(FpDevice *dev, gpointer user_data) {
-  fpi_ssm_jump_to_state(user_data, SCAN_STAGE_GET_IMG);
-}
-
 static gboolean preview_raw_file(const char *path, const Goodix55X4Pix *pixels,
                                  gint64 stamp, GError **error) {
   g_autofree char *header=g_strdup_printf("P5\n# %" G_GINT64_FORMAT "\n108 88\n4095\n",stamp);
@@ -1004,8 +1000,22 @@
     fpi_image_device_session_error(FP_IMAGE_DEVICE(dev), error);
     return TRUE;
   }
-  fpi_device_add_timeout(dev, 1, preview_next_frame, ssm, NULL);
-  return TRUE;
+  /* Recorded, not consumed.
+   *
+   * This used to schedule its own re-read and return TRUE, which kept the
+   * state machine in a preview loop that never reached the touch-confirmed
+   * branch below. That was fine while preview was only ever used for a
+   * research capture, and it is exactly wrong for enrolment: the window could
+   * show the finger and build its coverage map, but libfprint never advanced a
+   * single enrol stage, so a session that looked like it was working produced
+   * no enrolment at all.
+   *
+   * Returning FALSE hands the frame back to the caller, which already knows
+   * how to poll again when there is nothing there and how to confirm a touch
+   * when there is. Preview frames now arrive at the poll interval rather than
+   * the 1ms loop, so the window sees about 10 frames a second instead of 15.
+   */
+  return FALSE;
 }
 
 static void scan_on_read_img(FpDevice *dev, guint8 *data, guint16 len,
@@ -1028,7 +1038,13 @@
     return;
 
   if (diff < GOODIX55X4_FINGER_DETECT_DIFF_THRESHOLD) {
-    if (++self->poll_attempts >= GOODIX55X4_FINGER_DETECT_MAX_ATTEMPTS) {
+    /* Ten seconds of an empty sensor means something is wrong during a verify,
+     * where the prompt has already been shown and the finger should be there.
+     * During a preview the person is looking at a window, deciding where to
+     * press next, and pausing is what they are supposed to be doing - the
+     * session's own deadline above is what ends it. */
+    const gboolean previewing = g_getenv("GOODIX55X4_PREVIEW_PATH") != NULL;
+    if (!previewing && ++self->poll_attempts >= GOODIX55X4_FINGER_DETECT_MAX_ATTEMPTS) {
       /* Ten seconds of seeing nothing. Either nobody touched the sensor, or
        * the background we are subtracting already contains whatever is on it -
        * which is what happens if the very first calibration caught a finger.
